\title{Nobiin Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Nobiin (ISO 639-3 fia; also Halfawi, Mahas) is a Northern Nubian language of the Nilo-Saharan family, spoken in Sudan and Egypt, with two main dialects (Mahas and Fadija). Speaker estimates vary widely, from roughly 500,000 to 900,000. There is no standardised orthography: Latin, Arabic and a revived Old Nubian-based script are all in use.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
Search showed a publishing project planning four children's books in the Old Nubian-derived alphabet, and a lesson app. No official school curriculum or primer was found.
\begin{itemize}
\item Reading Nubian: books for a new generation (Middle East Eye): \url{https://acquiaprod.middleeasteye.net/node/217656}
\item Learn Nubian! (Nobiin), iPhone app with lessons and writing practice: \url{https://apps.apple.com/ph/app/learn-nubian-nobiin-premium/id6476841617}
\end{itemize}

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Nobiin language (cites Werner 1987, \emph{Grammatik des Nobiin}, the only published grammar found): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobiin_language}
\item Omniglot, Nobiin writing: \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/nobiin.htm}
\end{itemize}
No dedicated dictionary turned up in search.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found specifically; the Nubian Language Society's alphabet effort is described in the article below.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item New Lines Magazine, on Nubian efforts to revive the alphabet: \url{https://newlinesmag.com/spotlight/in-egypt-and-sudan-nubians-are-trying-to-bring-an-alphabet-back-from-the-dead/}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Nobiin is widely spoken but not taught in schools as far as found. Orthography is unsettled, and materials are community-led and diaspora-based. A curriculum would need an orthography decision first.

\end{document}
